WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.739
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday, August 10th,
2015, which means that the next US presidential

00:00:03.739 --> 00:00:06.540
election is just 454 days away.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.150
Hank, somewhere in the US, later tonight,
an egg will be fertilized, and that single

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:16.920
cell will grow into two and then four and
then much later, a baby will be born, an actual

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:22.160
human being will be thrust into the world
for the first time, and it will cry, presumably

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.400
over the state of American political discourse.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.630
Hank, that baby, which has not technically
been conceived yet, will be six months old

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:31.270
before we elect a president!

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:35.159
It will be laughing at its own farts by the
time we actually vote.

00:00:35.159 --> 00:00:38.640
So, Hank, campaign cycles are getting longer
around the world, but like, in the UK, people

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.340
don't start paying serious, proper attention
to the election until five weeks before it

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.030
happens, when the Queen dissolved parliament.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:50.800
In Canada, the 2015 campaign period is 78
days, among the longest in Canadian history,

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.070
and not coincidentally, also the most expensive.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.410
Ahh, but what I wouldn't give for 78 days.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:01.100
Hank, Germany, Italy, the Netherlands, Nigeria,
Australia, none of these places have campaign

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.300
cycles lasting longer than 100 days.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.950
Quick primer for non-Americans, in the US,
most states hold so-called "primary" elections

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.700
five to ten months before the proper election.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.640
In these primaries, Republican and Democratic
voters choose who they want to be their party's

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:18.560
candidate for president, and then those people
face each other in the general election.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.251
In the end, the whole process takes about
23 times longer than it does in the UK, and

00:01:22.251 --> 00:01:24.469
also costs 40 times more, so that's nice.

00:01:24.469 --> 00:01:25.469
How did this happen?

00:01:25.469 --> 00:01:28.220
Well, as with most things in American history,
it used to be much worse.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:32.240
For most of American history, the president
wasn't really elected democratically.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.719
For one thing, most people couldn't vote,
because most people were either women or not

00:01:36.719 --> 00:01:37.719
white.

00:01:37.719 --> 00:01:40.899
But even most white males had no say in their
party's presidential candidates, because those

00:01:40.899 --> 00:01:44.990
candidates were chosen either by Congress
or by powerful members of the party.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:48.740
But then, more people could vote and primaries
started to gain traction and in January of

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:54.530
1960, John F. Kennedy made history by declaring
his candidacy very early, almost a full year

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:57.890
before the election, and there's no way Kennedy
could have become president under the old

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:00.790
system, he was too...how do I put this delicately?

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:01.790
Catholic.

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:04.580
But actual voters liked him more than the
Democratic party establishment did, so he

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.840
became a nominee and eventually the President.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.640
And that's the argument for a long campaign
cycle, it allows underdogs to build momentum

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.190
and in doing so, makes the whole process more
democratic.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:18.000
But the length of presidential campaigns has
doubled since Kennedy, and costs have skyrocketed

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.480
and I struggle to see what we get for all
that time and money.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.670
There aren't a lot of serious policy discussions
at this point, actual clowns are being treated

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:30.470
as serious contenders for the presidency,
and instead of focusing on governing, people

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.360
have to focus entirely on campaigning.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.440
I mean, we're in the middle of a 22 month,
multi-billion dollar presidential campaign,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.390
but we haven't had a long-term bill to fund
highways in four years.

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:45.580
So yeah, I'm not paying attention to this
so-called presidential campaign at the moment.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:49.460
Now, I'm not criticizing those who do choose
to care about the US presidential campaign

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:53.740
now, there's lots of great organizing and
activism going on around the candidates, but

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:54.870
focus is elsewhere at the moment.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:59.540
It's on my kids and this great book that I
just read and the elections in Haiti two days

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:03.190
ago that finally occurred after three years
of delays.

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:04.190
Here's my pledge.

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:10.161
I will begin caring about the US presidential
election 100 days before the May 3rd primary

00:03:10.161 --> 00:03:11.370
here in Indiana.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:16.391
So if on January 23rd, 2016, Donald Trump
is still a candidate for president of the

00:03:16.391 --> 00:03:21.060
United States, I will think about Donald Trump
for the first time in my life, and I will

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.500
seek to understand his strengths and weaknesses
as a presidential candidate.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.450
Until then, I will remain blissfully ignorant.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:30.140
Hank, I will see you on Friday.

